title: Find in a panel
tags: GUI
kind: guide
aliases: find ctrl-f ctrl+f find-bar search-in-page find-in-page
summary: ctrl+f searches the text the focused panel is showing, in any panel type — what is on screen, not the repository.

<<key "find:open" Ctrl+f>> searches the text the focused panel is showing, as a
browser's find does for a page. It works in every panel type — this documentation
included — because the shell does it: a panel has nothing to provide.

|!Key |!Does |
|<<key "find:open" Ctrl+f>> |opens the find bar, above the command input, ready to type in. Pressed again, it goes back to the bar and selects what is in it, so that typing starts a new search |
|<<key "find:next" Enter>> / <<key "find:prev" Shift+Enter>> |the next / the previous match, going round at both ends |
|<<key "find:close" Escape>> |closes the bar and removes the highlighting |

The search runs as you type and ignores case. The bar gives the position of the
current match (`3/17`), or `no match`. Every match is highlighted, the current one
differently; it is scrolled into view only when it is not already comfortably
visible, so the text does not jump at each letter.

The text searched for is kept when the bar closes:
<<key "find:open" Ctrl+f>> then <<key "find:next" Enter>> resumes the last search.

!! What is searched

What the panel ''displays'':

* a match may run across formatting (a word partly in bold), and across a line break of the page's source;
* it never runs from one paragraph, row or cell into the next;
* hidden parts of the panel are not searched;
* neither is anything the panel has not loaded — the rows of a long list beyond those fetched.

So this is reading what is on screen, not asking the repository. To search a whole
repository, use the finder or the query of `metarecord-list`
(<<key "metarecord-list:focus finder" "/">>): they ask the daemon, and find
metarecords that are not displayed.

The search follows the panel: when the panel redraws, the matches are looked for
again in what it shows now; when another slot or workspace gets the focus, the
search moves to its panel.

Nothing is selected. To move a list's cursor onto a row by its name, see
[[Jumping to an entry]].

!! From a script

`find:open <text>` searches `text` at once, so a script can drive the bar with
`mf gui command 'find:open "…"'`, then `find:next`, `find:prev` and `find:close`.
`find:next` and `find:prev` do nothing while the bar is closed.

!! How it is done

The matches are painted with the CSS Custom Highlight API (`::highlight(mf-find)`,
`::highlight(mf-find-current)`, styled in the sheet every panel adopts): the panel's
content is not modified, so its handlers and its redraws are not disturbed by a
search running over them. The find bar's keys are ordinary bindings restricted to
the bar (`focus = "find"`, [[Keybindings]]).

